digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56945-0
  rankdir="TB";
  "C_clinical_base" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given the case that PRS are delivered to patients and the common pattern that delivery indicates clinical implementation, therefore PRS are advancing toward clinical use."];
  "C_gwas" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given the case that GWAS expanded and the common pattern that larger GWAS improve prediction, therefore GWAS expansion has contributed to PRS improvement."];
  "C_proposal_knowledge" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given the case that the large ancestrally-diverse All of Us cohort is available to compare high genetic risk classification for CAD, T2DM, MDD, and the common pattern that large diverse holdout data + integrative tools enable benchmarking, therefore they are suitable for testing classification stability."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study investigates, in the diverse All of Us cohort, how published polygenic risk scores for coronary artery disease, type 2 diabetes, and major depressive disorder vary in classifying individuals as high genetic risk, and evaluates whether the PRSmix method can reduce classification inconsistency and improve predictive accuracy.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When PRS are approaching clinical use, show individual-level high-risk classification inconsistency, and a diverse cohort plus an integrative PRS method are available, a study can benchmark inconsistency and test integration for prediction improvement."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) PRS predictive performance improvement over the past decade is an example of progress associated with advances in genetic-risk-score data and methods."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Development of clinically valid assays, workflows, and patient/physician materials for PRS reports in biobanks and studies supports the view that PRS are being prepared for clinical use."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Clinical trials incorporating PRS into medical decision-making and society statements on PRS utility indicate increasing clinical attention to PRS."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Because PRS are receiving increasing clinical attention, variability in high-risk classification is a key concern for PRS use in clinical decision-making."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) For PRS used in clinical risk classification, extreme-value reasoning suggests that models correlated on average may still differ in how they classify individuals at high-risk extremes."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Differences in training population, outcome definition, and statistical methodology are plausible sources of model differences in high-risk classification."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) When a new prediction model uses different predictors or modeling choices, significant reclassification of high-risk patients is a plausible explanation for changes in recommendations."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) Because current PRS reports assign categorical high-risk labels and different studies or companies use different scores for the same traits, PRS-based high-risk classification may be inconsistent across implementations."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) If PRS implementations can assign inconsistent categorical high-risk labels, then the largely untested variability of individual-level high genetic risk classification is a study gap."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Bayesian approaches are one method-level advance that can improve PRS construction by using prior knowledge about genetic architecture, relatedness, linkage disequilibrium, and cross-population genetic effects."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) Prior emphasis on population-level metrics rather than consistency in individual clinical reports helps explain why individual-level PRS agreement remains an unresolved benchmarking gap."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) Limited large, diverse holdout datasets have further precluded individual-level benchmarking of PRS agreement, reinforcing the unresolved gap in testing high-risk classification consistency."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) As GWAS size and statistical methods continue to generate new PRS, the unresolved benchmarking gap creates a need for frameworks that can update PRS use without adding confusion."];
  "R24" [label="(0,0,0) Given the need to update and aggregate available PRS while managing correlated information, an integrative scoring framework is required."];
  "R26" [label="(0,0,0) A suitable research idea is to use the large ancestrally diverse All of Us cohort to compare high genetic risk classification for CAD, T2DM, and MDD across PRS and to evaluate PRSmix integration for improved prediction."];
  "R27" [label="(0,0,0) The PREVENT model predicting lower risk than PCE and reclassifying about half of US adults to lower categories is an example consistent with new-model reclassification affecting risk categories."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Incorporating multi-ancestry and multi-trait GWAS data is a specific avenue by which expanding GWAS resources can improve PRS prediction in diverse ancestral groups."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Differences in source GWAS, PRS method, and training dataset can make successive PRS iterations for the same trait capture partly non-interchangeable information."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Delivery of PRS by third-party companies and healthcare laboratories for CAD, diabetes, cancers, and other diseases provides evidence that PRS are moving toward clinical implementation."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) The eMERGE consortium's development of PRS reports for 10 diseases to return to participants is another example of PRS translation toward clinical genomic risk assessment."];
  "implicit_common_clinical" [label="(0,0,0) The common pattern is that increasing delivery by companies, labs, consortia and trials indicates advancing clinical implementation of PRS."];
  "implicit_common_gwas" [label="(0,0,0) The common pattern is that larger GWAS improve prediction."];
  "implicit_common_improve" [label="(0,0,0) The common pattern is that predictive performances of genetic risk scores improve over time with research advances in data and methods."];
  "implicit_knowledge_reclass" [label="(0,0,0) The knowledge that introduction of new models with different predictors (training, outcome, methodology) leads to significant reclassification."];
  "orig_sent1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently there is the case that PRS have improved in predictive performances over the past decade."];
  "orig_sent10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently there is the case that new clinical trials are incorporating PRS into medical decision-making and societies have released statements on utility."];
  "orig_sent11" [label="(11,0,0) If consistency in risk classification is important for clinical decision-making, then risk prediction models for complex diseases inherently have variability in high-risk classification."];
  "orig_sent12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently there is the case that differences stem from training population, outcome definition, and underlying statistical methodology."];
  "orig_sent13" [label="(13,0,0) If different risk prediction models are correlated at their mean, then extreme value theory suggests they are inherently likely to differ in classifying risk in the extremes."];
  "orig_sent14" [label="(14,0,0) Currently there is the phenomenon that introduction of new models often leads to significant reclassification of high-risk patients, raising questions about altering recommendations."];
  "orig_sent15" [label="(15,0,0) Currently there is the phenomenon that PREVENT predicts lower risk than PCE and reclassifies about half of US adults to lower categories."];
  "orig_sent17" [label="(17,0,0) Currently there is the phenomenon that current PRS reports provide categorical high risk and different studies/companies use different scores for the same traits."];
  "orig_sent18" [label="(18,0,0) Currently there is the case that advances in GWAS size and statistical methods fuel new PRS, requiring a framework for updating without confusion."];
  "orig_sent19" [label="(19,0,0) Currently there is the phenomenon that variability of individual-level high genetic risk classification using different PRS remains largely untested (and no consensus PRS exists)."];
  "orig_sent2" [label="(2,0,0) If Bayesian approaches using prior knowledge about genetic architecture, relatedness of individuals, linkage disequilibrium patterns, and genetic effects across populations are used, then the assignment of variant weights and PRS performance is improved."];
  "orig_sent20" [label="(20,0,0) Currently there is the case that previous efforts focused on population-level metrics rather than consistency in individual clinical reports."];
  "orig_sent21" [label="(21,0,0) Currently there is the case that limited large holdout diverse datasets precluded individual-level benchmarking of PRS agreement."];
  "orig_sent22" [label="(22,0,0) If there is a need to aggregate and incorporate orthogonal data from available PRS while overcoming correlation, then integrative scoring is required."];
  "orig_sent23" [label="(23,0,0) Currently there is the case that the large ancestrally-diverse All of Us cohort is available to compare high genetic risk classification for CAD, T2DM, MDD."];
  "orig_sent24" [label="(24,0,0) The knowledge that PRSmix is a tool that agnostically integrates information across several PRS for a given trait to improve prediction accuracy for a target population."];
  "orig_sent3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently there is the case that GWAS have continued to expand in size to millions of participants."];
  "orig_sent4" [label="(4,0,0) If multi-ancestry and multi-trait GWAS data are incorporated, then PRS prediction is improved in diverse ancestral groups."];
  "orig_sent5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently there is the case that each PRS iteration for a given trait captures unique information contingent on source GWAS, method, and training dataset."];
  "orig_sent6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently there is the case that PRS are increasingly being delivered to patients."];
  "orig_sent7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently there is the case that third-party companies and healthcare laboratories are delivering PRS for CAD, diabetes, cancers and other diseases."];
  "orig_sent8" [label="(8,0,0) Currently there is the case that the eMERGE consortium has developed PRS reports for 10 diseases to return to participants as part of genomic risk assessment."];
  "orig_sent9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently there is the case that clinically valid assays, workflows, and patient/physician materials have been developed for PRS reports in biobanks and studies."];
  "implicit_common_gwas" -> "C_gwas" [label="induction-common"];
